{
  "_meta": {
    "language": "da",
    "status": "draft-for-native-review"
  },
  "grammar": {
    "time_format": "24h",
    "decimal_hour_idioms": [
      {"phrase": "kvart over", "minutes": 15},
      {"phrase": "halv", "minutes": -30},
      {"phrase": "kvart i", "minutes": -15}
    ],
    "conjunction": "og",
    "notes": "PARSER TRAP: Danish 'halv' counts DOWN to the named hour (like German), not up. 'halv tre' = 2:30, NOT 3:30. So 'halv X' = X minus 30 minutes. 'kvart over to' = 2:15, 'kvart i tre' = 2:45. Spoken Danish commonly uses 12h clock-face idioms ('halv tre') even though written/digital Danish is 24h; 'om eftermiddagen/aftenen' may be added to disambiguate AM/PM. 'klokken' (often shortened 'kl.') precedes a bare hour number."
  },
